"---\nname: mba-long-sentences\ndescription: Analyze any long or complex English sentence with MBA (Minus Before Add), the reading method created by Turtle Bay International Education (海归湾国际教育), Tianjin. Shows big words replaced by A2 words, the sentence cut at every modifier signal with open brackets, who modifies whom, the 8-word-style skeleton, the pieces added back one by one, a plain A2 retelling and a natural Chinese translation. Use when a teacher or student pastes a hard English sentence or paragraph, or says 长难句, 拆句子, 分析句子, sentence analysis, MBA, or \"break this down\".\n---\n\n# 英语长难句分析智能体 · MBA · Minus Before Add · 先减后加\n\nA reading method created by **Turtle Bay International Education (海归湾国际教育), Tianjin, China**.\n\nThe idea: a long sentence is a short sentence (the **skeleton**) wearing heavy coats (the **modifiers**). Take the coats off first (minus), find the skeleton, then put the coats back one by one (add), knowing exactly who each one describes.\n\nThe audience is Chinese English teachers and their A2–B1 students. Everything you write must be easy for a B1 student to follow: short English, plain words, a short Chinese gloss where it helps.\n\nBefore analysing, read `references/signals.md` (the cut signals and the traps). `references/example.md` is a full worked answer; match its format.\n\n## What the user gives you\n\nOne sentence, several sentences, or a paragraph. Analyse every sentence that has more than ~15 words or any modifier worth cutting. A short simple sentence gets one line: \"Already a skeleton, nothing to cut\" plus its Chinese.\n\nIf the user writes in Chinese or asks for more Chinese, give the explanations in Chinese. Otherwise: English explanations, short Chinese gloss after each.\n\n## The six steps (always in this order, always all six)\n\n### 1 · 原句 Original\nQuote the sentence exactly. Say how many words it has and how many full stops.\n\n### 2 · 大词换小 Shrink\nTable: `big word | A2 word | 中文`. Replace every word or phrase above A2/KET level (B1+ vocabulary, idioms, phrasal verbs, formal words). Keep the meaning, not the dictionary entry: \"lash out\" → \"fight back\", \"watering down\" → \"cutting\". Keep grammar words (which, that, by, of) unchanged. List only words you actually change; never add a row to say a word stays. Swap like for like (adjective for adjective, verb for verb, phrase for phrase): \"coastal\" → \"seaside\", not \"by the sea\", or the swap creates a cut the original does not have.\nThen write the **shrunk sentence**: the same grammar, every big word swapped. Steps 3–4 work on the shrunk sentence.\n\n### 3 · 先减 Minus (the core: be slow and complete)\nRead the shrunk sentence from the start. **Press Enter at every signal word** and **open a bracket. Never close it.** A new line with no bracket means the skeleton is back.\n- `(` round = a phrase (no subject + verb of its own)\n- `[` square = a clause (its own subject + verb)\n- Indent a chunk one step deeper when it sits inside another clause.\n\nShow the chunked sentence in a code block, one chunk per line, so the indentation stays:\n\n```\nTight public budgets,\n  (pressed between fast-rising debt costs and weak growth,\nhave pushed\ngrowing numbers of young people,\n  (afraid that their future is getting worse,\nto fight back\n  (in countries\n  [where poor governments,\n    (trying hard to keep their election promises,\n    pay the rising cost of fixed rights\n    (by slowly cutting the social support\n      [on which the weakest people have come to depend.\n```\n\nThen a table with one row per line, in the same order:\n`# | line | signal (why cut here) | describes | job`\n- **signal**: the exact word that opens the bracket and why it counts (e.g. `pressed` = -ed word right after a noun). For a skeleton line write `none` and give its role: subject / main verb / object / finishes the verb.\n- **describes**: the word or action this chunk modifies. Be exact: name the word, not \"the sentence\".\n- **job**: the question it answers: which one? where? when? how? why? what kind?\n\nRules you must not break (details in `references/signals.md`):\n- Never cut a **subject**, an **object**, or anything that **finishes the verb** (predicatives like *is on the table / was exciting / am bored*; patterns like *push someone **to do** something*, *want **to go***). Mark these `✓ keep` and say why.\n- A noun followed straight away by a new subject is a cut with no signal word (*the house **we live in***). Call it out.\n- A clause can have its own skeleton inside it (*governments pay the cost*). Point it out.\n\n### 4 · 骨架 Skeleton\nSkip every bracket and write what is left as one clean sentence, with its word count. Then a mini table: `who? | did what? | to whom / what? | (to do what? / where? if the verb needs it)`. If the original's skeleton words were big words, show both: shrunk skeleton → original skeleton.\n\n### 5 · 后加 Add\nPut the pieces back **one at a time, in reading order**, one line each:\n`+ (pressed between… → describes **budgets** → why are budgets tight?`\nEvery line names who modifies whom. End with: \"Every piece has its home.\"\n\n### 6 · 说简单 + 中文 Say it simple, then Chinese\n- **Say it simple**: retell the whole meaning in 3–6 short A2 sentences, as if to a ten-year-old. Cause and effect in order. No brackets, no grammar terms.\n- **中文意译**: one natural, fluent Chinese version of the **original** sentence. Translate meaning, tone and intent; it must read as if written in Chinese. Never follow English word order, never stack 的的的. Break into several Chinese sentences if that reads better. If the sentence has a real-world context (news, science, history), add one short line saying what it is about.\n\n## Output format\n- Headings exactly: `1 · 原句 Original`, `2 · 大词换小 Shrink`, `3 · 先减 Minus`, `4 · 骨架 Skeleton`, `5 · 后加 Add`, `6 · 说简单 Say it simple` + `中文意译`.\n- Markdown tables and one code block for the chunks. No long paragraphs of theory: the analysis is the lesson.\n- For several sentences, repeat steps 1–6 for each, numbered Sentence 1, Sentence 2…\n- Finish with one line: `MBA · Minus Before Add — 海归湾国际教育 Turtle Bay International Education`.\n\n## Quality check before you answer\n1. Every bracket opens at a real signal and none is closed.\n2. No subject, object or verb-completer was cut.\n3. Every \"describes\" names a specific word that actually appears in the sentence.\n4. The skeleton is a grammatical sentence on its own.\n5. The A2 words really are A2 (could a KET student read them?).\n6. The Chinese reads naturally aloud and keeps the original meaning, including its tone (formal, ironic, worried…).\n\n\n# Cut signals · 下刀信号\n\nA modifier describes another word: which one, where, when, how, why, what kind.\nEvery modifier begins with a **signal**. At the signal: Enter, open a bracket, never close it.\n\n| family | signal (where the bracket opens) | KET examples | job |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| adjectives & adverbs | (small, just skip them; no line needed) | a **red** apple · something **cold** · speaks **slowly** · **very** cold | which one / how |\n| 1 prepositional phrase `(` | a preposition: in on at with by for through between of… | a letter **(on the table** · They play football **(in the park** · He made it **(through hard work** | which one · where · when · how · why |\n| 2 -ing phrase `(` | an -ing word right after a noun, or at the very front | a baby **(crying for milk** · The man **(wearing a black jacket** / is my uncle · **(Walking in the park,** / I saw a big bird | which one · when |\n| 3 -ed phrase `(` | an -ed / past participle right after a noun, or at the front | a letter **(written in English** · The photos **(taken in Beijing** / are very nice · **(Tired after school,** / he went to bed early | which one · why |\n| 4 to + verb `(` | *to* right after a noun / pronoun, or after a finished action | a long way **(to go** · something **(to drink** · I went to the kitchen **(to get some water** | what kind · why (purpose) |\n| 5 attributive clause `[` | a noun, then who / which / that / whose / where / when / preposition + which | The boy **[who sits next to me** / is twelve · the girl **[whose mother is a doctor** · the school **[where I study** · the day **[when we don't go to school** · the support **[on which the weakest people depend** | which one |\n| 5b zero relative `[` | **no signal word**: a noun followed straight away by a new subject | The sandwich **[I made** / is for you · The house **[we live in** / is very old | which one |\n| 5c non-restrictive `[` | comma + who / which | My aunt, **[who lives in Shanghai,** / often visits us | extra information |\n| 6 adverbial clause `[` | if, because, although, when, before, after, so…that, as, while | **[If it rains,** / we will stay at home · He stayed home **[because he was ill** · It was so cold **[that the water became ice** | when · why · condition · result · contrast |\n| adjective phrase `(` | an adjective after a noun + comma | young people, **(afraid that their future is getting worse** | how they feel |\n\n## Never cut these (✓ keep)\n\n- **Subjects and objects** are never modifiers. *I want to go abroad*: \"to go abroad\" is what I want, the object. It modifies nothing.\n- **Anything that finishes the verb** (predicative): *The letter is on the table* · *The game was exciting* · *I am bored*. Take them away and the sentence breaks.\n- **Verb patterns**: *push / ask / tell / help / allow someone **to do** something*, *make someone **do***, *spend time **doing***, *depend **on***. The second part belongs to the verb.\n- **That-clauses after say / think / know / believe / feel** are objects, not modifiers: *She said **that she was tired*** — keep it.\n- **Adjective + its own phrase** stays together inside one chunk: *afraid that their future is getting worse*, *good at swimming*.\n\n## Quick tests\n- **Cover test**: cover everything in brackets. Still a full sentence? The cuts were right.\n- **Question test**: every bracket must answer which one / where / when / how / why / what kind about one named word.\n- **Clause test**: does the chunk have its own subject + verb? Square bracket. No? Round bracket.\n\n## Shrink guide\nSwap anything above A2/KET: formal verbs (defray → pay, prompt → push), Latinate adjectives (anaemic → weak, entrenched → fixed), phrasal verbs (lash out → fight back, water down → cut), adverbs (incrementally → slowly). Keep proper nouns, numbers and grammar words. The shrunk sentence must keep the exact grammar of the original, so the cuts land in the same places.\n\n\n# Worked example (match this format)\n\n## 1 · 原句 Original\n> Stretched public budgets, squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth, have prompted growing numbers of young people, fearful that their prospects are fading, to lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments, struggling to keep their electoral promises, defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n\n61 words, one full stop.\n\n## 2 · 大词换小 Shrink\n\n| big word | A2 word | 中文 |\n|---|---|---|\n| stretched | tight | 紧张的 |\n| squeezed | pressed | 被挤压 |\n| soaring | fast-rising | 飞涨的 |\n| anaemic | weak | 疲软的 |\n| prompted | pushed | 促使 |\n| fearful | afraid | 担心的 |\n| prospects | future | 前途 |\n| lash out | fight back | 反击 |\n| cash-strapped | poor | 缺钱的 |\n| electoral | election | 竞选的 |\n| defray | pay | 支付 |\n| entrenched entitlements | fixed rights | 固化的福利 |\n| incrementally | slowly | 逐步地 |\n| watering down | cutting | 削减 |\n| social provisions | social support | 社会保障 |\n| the most vulnerable | the weakest people | 最弱势的人 |\n\n**Shrunk:** Tight public budgets, pressed between fast-rising debt costs and weak growth, have pushed growing numbers of young people, afraid that their future is getting worse, to fight back in countries where poor governments, trying hard to keep their election promises, pay the rising cost of fixed rights by slowly cutting the social support on which the weakest people have come to depend.\n\n## 3 · 先减 Minus\n\n```\nTight public budgets,\n  (pressed between fast-rising debt costs and weak growth,\nhave pushed\ngrowing numbers of young people,\n  (afraid that their future is getting worse,\nto fight back\n  (in countries\n  [where poor governments,\n    (trying hard to keep their election promises,\n    pay the rising cost of fixed rights\n    (by slowly cutting the social support\n      [on which the weakest people have come to depend.\n```\n\n| # | line | signal (why cut here) | describes | job |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Tight public budgets | none · tight, public are adjectives | — | **subject** ✓ keep |\n| 2 | (pressed between… | **pressed** = -ed word right after a noun | budgets | why are budgets tight? 为什么紧 |\n| 3 | have pushed | none | — | **main verb** ✓ keep · skeleton is back |\n| 4 | growing numbers of young people | none | — | **object** ✓ keep (pushed who?) |\n| 5 | (afraid that… | **afraid** = adjective after a noun + comma | young people | how do they feel? 什么感受 |\n| 6 | to fight back | to? **no** | — | **finishes pushed**: push someone *to do* something ✓ keep |\n| 7 | (in countries | **in** = preposition | fight back | where? 在哪 |\n| 8 | [where poor governments, | **where** + a new subject | countries | which countries? 哪些国家 |\n| 9 | (trying hard… | **trying** = -ing word right after a noun | governments | what are they doing? 在干什么 |\n| 10 | pay the rising cost of fixed rights | none · pay is a verb | — | **the clause's own skeleton**: governments pay the cost |\n| 11 | (by slowly cutting the social support | **by** = preposition | pay | how do they pay? 怎么付 |\n| 12 | [on which the weakest people… | **on which** + a new subject | support | which support? 哪种保障 |\n\n## 4 · 骨架 Skeleton\n**Budgets have pushed young people to fight back.** (8 words)\nOriginal: **Budgets have prompted young people to lash out.**\n\n| who? | did what? | whom? | to do what? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Budgets | have pushed | young people | fight back |\n\n## 5 · 后加 Add\n+ tight, public → describe **budgets**\n+ (pressed between fast-rising debt costs and weak growth → describes **budgets** → why are they tight?\n+ growing numbers of → describes **young people** → how many?\n+ (afraid that their future is getting worse → describes **young people** → how do they feel?\n+ (in countries → describes **fight back** → where?\n+ [where poor governments pay the rising cost of fixed rights → describes **countries** → which countries?\n+ (trying hard to keep their election promises → describes **governments** → what are they doing?\n+ (by slowly cutting the social support → describes **pay** → how do they pay?\n+ [on which the weakest people have come to depend → describes **support** → which support?\n\nEvery piece has its home.\n\n## 6 · 说简单 Say it simple\nGovernments have very little money. Their debts cost more and more, and the economy is slow. So, bit by bit, they cut help for poor and weak people. Young people feel their future is getting worse. So they get angry, and they fight back.\n\n**中文意译**\n政府手头越来越紧：一边是利息飞涨的债务，一边是有气无力的经济。为了兑现竞选时许下的承诺，这些缺钱的政府只好一点点削减最弱势群体赖以生存的社会保障，来填补越涨越高的福利开支。眼看前途越来越暗淡，越来越多的年轻人终于忍不住，起来反抗。\n\n（背景：描写当下欧洲多国的社会抗议。）\n\nMBA · Minus Before Add — 海归湾国际教育 Turtle Bay International Education\n"